{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "סמך שבת ללא תשא", :translit "samakh shabbat lo tisa", :gloss "[he] juxtaposed Sabbath to you shall not take"} {:he "שבשניהם נאמר חלול", :translit "shebishneihem ne'emar chillul", :gloss "that in both of them desecration is stated"} {:he "לא תשבעו בשמי לשקר", :translit "lo tishbe'u bishmi lashaker", :gloss "you shall not swear by My name falsely"} {:he "וחללת", :translit "vechilalta", :gloss "and you shall profane [it]"} {:he "מחלליה מות יומת", :translit "mechaleleiha mot yumat", :gloss "one who desecrates it shall be put to death"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Juxtaposition of adjacent topics: the Sabbath command precedes the oath command, signaling a shared theme of chillul (desecration)."}], :frame {:text "The broader principle that juxtaposition (semikhut) of verses signals a thematic connection is a standard hermeneutical method. The specific root ח-ל-ל (ch-l-l) appears in the context of Sabbath desecration in Exodus 31:14 ('mechaleleiha mot yumat') and in the context of profaning God's name in Leviticus 19:12 ('lo tishav'u… vechilalta'). The legal principle that one who desecrates the Sabbath is liable to the death penalty is stated in Exodus 31:14 and expounded in tractate Sanhedrin.", :sources ["Exodus 31:14" "Leviticus 19:12" "Sanhedrin 65b-66a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Juxtaposition of adjacent topics: the Sabbath command precedes the oath command, signaling a shared theme of chillul (desecration).", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:34:33.811208065Z", :model "glm-5.2"}